const COURSE_21_QUIZZES_DEF = {
  c21q_l1:{ title:'Quiz: Before it happens', qs:[
    { q:'Why does incident response have a fixed order of phases?',
      opts:['because otherwise the incident could not be reported to management and the insurer, which explicitly requires the phase order','because in a crisis people tend to clean up before they know what happened – destroying the ground for the next step','because each phase must be finished completely before the next may start, otherwise the procedure repeats'],
      correct:1, why:'The phases run preparation, detection, containment, remediation, recovery, lessons learned. They loop back and overlap – what matters is knowing which you are in and its goal.' },
    { q:'Which thing can no longer be added during a crisis?',
      opts:['the list of phone contacts for application vendors, the connectivity provider and management including deputies','logs from before the incident: what was not written down cannot be added, and the timeline has a gap there','the decision whether the compromised server is disconnected or keeps running under observation'],
      correct:1, why:'Contacts and decisions can be made on the spot, logs cannot. So a log server is the first item of preparation – along with a network map and configuration backups.' },
    { q:'Why are roles separated during an incident?',
      opts:['so it can later be determined exactly who on the team caused the incident and personal responsibility assigned','because they interfere: whoever investigates should not also decide about service or answer management questions','because under the applicable rules each role may be held only by a person with special training and certification'],
      correct:1, why:'There are three roles: who decides, who investigates, who communicates. When alone, switch between them deliberately and in turns – not all at once.' },
    { q:'What is escalation in incident response?',
      opts:['a worsening of the incident where the attack spreads from one device to further segments and starts threatening servers','a threshold set in advance beyond which you call further: longer duration, personal data, several segments, unknown scope','a procedure by which the compromised device is gradually unplugged from parts of the network until it stands alone'],
      correct:1, why:'Escalation is not an admission of incompetence but an ordinary step. A threshold set in advance spares you deliberation in the middle of the night, when decisions are worst.' }
  ]},
  c21q_l2:{ title:'Quiz: Detection', qs:[
    { q:'Why is a decoy (honeypot) the cleanest signal in a network?',
      opts:['because it watches all network traffic and can pick out attacks that other tools do not have in their signatures','because nobody has a legitimate reason to talk to it, so a connection there is almost never a false alarm','because it records all connections into its own log, which an attacker has no way to reach'],
      correct:1, why:'The first report comes from a user, a sensor, monitoring or a decoy. Each source has a different speed and precision – so you do not rely on one.' },
    { q:'What is triage after an alert arrives?',
      opts:['immediately disconnecting every device the alert concerns, so that the spread is stopped before it can really get going','a quick sorting into false alarm, trifle and real incident by whether it is real, how far it reaches and what is at stake','a detailed analysis of the attack down to its author, done before anything in the network is changed'],
      correct:1, why:'Without triage you either burn out on false alarms (alert fatigue) or a real breach sinks among hundreds of lines. Content decides, not the message color.' },
    { q:'Why is severity not the same as loudness?',
      opts:['because tools assign severity levels at random, so their rating carries no informational value at all during an investigation of an incident','because an alert labeled critical is often noise, while an ‘informational’ line may be a breach’s first step','because critical alerts are sent to syslog more slowly than others and arrive with a delay of several minutes'],
      correct:1, why:'In triage you look at what happened and what it concerns, not at how loudly the tool says it. The level is a hint, not a verdict.' },
    { q:'How long should you verify before declaring an incident?',
      opts:['as long as it takes to be absolutely certain of the scope and the author, because a false alarm is always worse than a late reaction','minutes, not hours: a short check guards against a false alarm, a long analysis only gives the attacker time','not at all, because every sensor alert is by its nature a confirmation of an attack genuinely underway'],
      correct:1, why:'You verify with a second source, time and context. But verification must not become a reason to do nothing – with a serious, confirmed suspicion do not postpone containment.' }
  ]},
  c21q_l3:{ title:'Quiz: Containment', qs:[
    { q:'What does quarantine (Isolate) do to a device?',
      opts:['it removes malicious code from it and returns its settings to the last known state from a configuration backup','it leaves it physically in place, but the network cuts it off: no talking to neighbors or outward, and nobody to it','it moves it into a special VLAN where devices may exchange only a limited amount of traffic among themselves'],
      correct:1, why:'Quarantine shrinks the blast radius at once, but has a price: an isolated user or server does not work and the attacker learns you found them.' },
    { q:'When does watching a while instead of isolating make sense?',
      opts:['always, because isolation is irreversible and harms company operations more than any attack merely underway so far','with a quiet slow breach, when you have monitoring, an operator, a preset time limit and a trigger for immediate isolation','never, because every compromised device must be disconnected within seconds of discovery or the procedure is invalid'],
      correct:1, why:'Isolation saves the network, watching saves knowledge. When it spreads within minutes, data is at stake, or you see exfiltration, isolate at once.' },
    { q:'What does isolating one device not solve?',
      opts:['stopping traffic from the compromised device to its neighbors in the same segment and to all the servers in the network','the entry cause, further already-compromised devices, and persistent access: an account, a changed rule, a redirected record','cutting the compromised device off from the internet, so that traffic to foreign addresses stops leaving it entirely'],
      correct:1, why:'So after isolation you do not pause but go straight into the investigation. Quarantine stopped the clock – it bought you room, not completion.' },
    { q:'When does a compromised device return to the network?',
      opts:['as soon as it stops showing suspicious activity and the sensor raises no new alert about it for a while','after remediation: cleaned or reinstalled, with changed passwords and a check that it will not start again','after a period set by management according to how much the device is needed for the company to run'],
      correct:1, why:'A device going quiet does not mean it is clean – it may just be waiting. Without removing the cause and persistence, it starts again on return.' }
  ]},
  c21q_l4:{ title:'Quiz: Investigation', qs:[
    { q:'Which three points do you look for on an incident timeline?',
      opts:['the attacker’s name, their country of origin and the tool used, because without these an incident cannot be closed','the first occurrence, the turning point and the last activity: when it began, when it began to harm, is it live','the number of affected devices, the number of lost files and the number of hours ordinary service was down'],
      correct:1, why:'The first occurrence tends to be earlier than you thought – the alert is only a consequence. And holes are noted: unrecorded does not mean it did not happen.' },
    { q:'How is a breach recognized in the connections overview?',
      opts:['by the total volume of data moved, because an attack always sharply raises traffic on all links at once','by a new ‘who talks to whom’ pair that was not there before: a computer talking to a server it never touched','by missing connections, because a compromised device stops communicating with all its usual counterparts'],
      correct:1, why:'So knowing the usual flows from calm times is valuable. From them you derive the real blast radius: where the attacker actually got, not where they could.' },
    { q:'Why are your own steps written down during an incident?',
      opts:['for management reporting, which needs proof of how many work hours the incident consumed and who exactly worked them','so that two days later you can tell what the attacker caused and what you did yourself while intervening and cleaning up','because from them the original settings of all affected devices are automatically restored'],
      correct:1, why:'Both findings and actions are recorded, with times. And evidence is copied, not edited, and stored away from the compromised device – the chain of custody principle.' },
    { q:'Why should you not start with cleanup?',
      opts:['because under established procedures only an external forensic specialist may clean up, and only after the investigation ends','because a reboot, a reinstall or deleting a file are irreversible and often destroy just what you would need in an hour','because cleanup takes longer than the investigation and needlessly extends the time company operations are halted'],
      correct:1, why:'Record first, clean up after. The timeline and flows are the investigation’s most valuable output – the scope, remediation and post-mortem follow from them.' }
  ]},
  c21q_l5:{ title:'Quiz: Remediation and recovery', qs:[
    { q:'How do you tell you fixed a symptom instead of the cause?',
      opts:['by the fix taking less time than had been expected and requiring no change at all to the firewall or the server settings','by the question ‘if the attacker tried the same again, would they get through?’ – if yes, you fixed a symptom','by alerts ceasing after the fix and no new suspicious lines appearing in syslog any more'],
      correct:1, why:'Causes tend to be boring and repetitive: an exposed service, a missing update, a shared password, a broad rule, missing segmentation. The remediation is boring too.' },
    { q:'Why are services brought back in parts after an incident?',
      opts:['because devices must not be powered on simultaneously after a longer outage, to avoid overloading the power and the network links','because if you turn everything on at once and something breaks you do not know what – one by one you see it at once and check the logs','because the power-on order is set by the manufacturer and breaking it may stop devices joining the network correctly'],
      correct:1, why:'After each return check the logs and the connections overview. If the old suspicious pair reappears, the remediation was incomplete and you go back.' },
    { q:'What is the N-1 test for during recovery after an incident?',
      opts:['it verifies all devices have the latest updates installed after the incident and an identical settings version','it shows what stops working when individual elements fail – whether a single path is left after your intervention','it measures how much link throughput dropped during the incident and how long restoring it takes'],
      correct:1, why:'During an incident something is unplugged, blocked or rerouted. The network then runs, but on one path only – which the N-1 test and topology check reveal.' },
    { q:'When is an incident really closed?',
      opts:['as soon as service runs and nobody complains, since from the users’ view the situation is resolved and further steps are pointless','when the cause is removed, the scope known, service verified by someone else and a record exists to hand on','after thirty days have passed since the last suspicious event recorded by any device in the network'],
      correct:1, why:'If any condition fails, the incident is not closed – merely quiet. And what could not be established is honestly recorded as residual risk.' }
  ]},
  c21q_l6:{ title:'Quiz: Lessons learned', qs:[
    { q:'Why is a post-mortem done without looking for blame?',
      opts:['because with network incidents the culprit can never be reliably determined anyway and attempts at it only drag the meeting out','because when blame is sought people stop reporting mistakes and you learn of the next incident much later, thus more expensively','because decisions on personal responsibility belong solely to management and the technical team must not interfere'],
      correct:1, why:'You do not look for who caused it, but for what made it possible. The outline is short: what happened, how we found out, what we did, what we will change.' },
    { q:'Which question is the most valuable in a post-mortem?',
      opts:['how much money the incident cost including lost profit, technician overtime and replacement hardware','could it have been found earlier? The answer shows where to add logging or where to build a decoy','who on the team was fastest during the incident and how their approach can be passed to the others'],
      correct:1, why:'Time to detection is the one figure you directly influence with monitoring. Each measure then gets one name and one date – otherwise a post-mortem is just talk.' },
    { q:'Which post-incident measure shrinks the blast radius most effectively?',
      opts:['raising link throughput between the switch and the firewall, so traffic is not held up at a network bottleneck during an attack','segmentation: a boundary between parts of the network, so one captured machine does not reach everywhere','regular password changes for all users on a plan set by management together with the security team'],
      correct:1, why:'Besides segmentation you narrow overly broad rules and add monitoring. And: few changes, but finished – half-done segmentation is worse than none.' },
    { q:'Why is incident response rehearsed in advance?',
      opts:['because without regular drills you cannot obtain the certification insurers require when arranging cyber-risk cover','because a procedure nobody ever walked is not a procedure but a document – and a drill finds missing contacts and logs','because a drill tests the performance of network elements under the load an incident usually puts on the links'],
      correct:1, why:'A half-hour drill – ‘the server is compromised, now what?’ – reveals gaps more reliably than an audit. Incident response is not trained during an incident.' }
  ]}
};
